<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capitalism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production, production for profit and the accumulation of capital through competitive markets and wage labour.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Capitalism in its modern form is traced to agrarian capitalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. Capital had existed on a small scale for centuries in merchant, renting and lending activities. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> promulgated policies such as free trade and banking, and their use of Indo-Arabic numerals facilitated bookkeeping; these innovations reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>.</p><p>In 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the manorial system broke down, land became concentrated in fewer landlords and workers were increasingly employed in a money-based economy instead of as serfs. From the 16th to the 18th centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a> prevailed, a system of trade for profit, though goods were still largely produced by non-capitalist methods. Most scholars see it as the origin of the modern system, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-polanyi">Karl Polanyi</a> held that capitalism required generalised markets for land, labour and money, and so could not be said to exist before 1834, when a competitive labour market was established in England.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production and its use to obtain profit. Its elements include private property, the profit motive, capital accumulation, competitive markets and wage labour. Its forms, among them laissez-faire, state and welfare capitalism, differ in their degree of free markets, public ownership and regulation. There is no universally agreed definition.</p><p>The term capitalist, meaning an owner of capital, dates to the mid-17th century. Louis Blanc in 1850 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a> in 1861 are credited with the first modern use of capitalism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> wrote of the capitalist mode of production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a> (1867). Because socialist critics coined the word, Robert Hessen called it a term of disparagement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> of the 18th century made capitalism the dominant mode of production, characterised by factory work and a complex division of labour. It spread across the world in the 19th and 20th centuries. In the 19th century it was largely unregulated; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it became more regulated through Keynesianism, followed from the 1980s by a return of less regulated capitalism termed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoliberalism">neoliberalism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Capitalism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of Genoa built a commercial empire from the Black Sea to the Atlantic, pioneered the contracts, insurance and public debt of European <b>capitalism</b>, and through its bankers financed the Spanish Empire, making a small Ligurian city one of the makers of the early modern world economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Diệm adopted Personalism, a doctrine presented as a third way between Marxist collectivism and Western <b>capitalism</b>, gave key posts to his family, with Ngô Đình Nhu gaining authority over the military, police and intelligence, and built a personality cult through the Ministry of Information and Youth and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Capitalism</b> and Slavery of 1944 the Trinidadian historian Eric Williams, later his country’s first prime minister, argued that Britain abolished the trade and slavery when the West Indian sugar economy was declining and no longer served British capitalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It does not adopt Marxism wholesale, but its use of concepts such as class struggle and its critique of global <b>capitalism</b> led to significant controversy within the Church.</blockquote><a href="/movement/liberation-theology">Liberation theology · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859) undermined religious certainty and the idea of human uniqueness, and Karl Marx’s Das Kapital (1867) argued that <b>capitalism</b> was full of contradictions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Pan-Africanists also differed over whether the movement was defined by race, uniting Black people everywhere, or by the continent, including the Arab states of North Africa; over <b>capitalism</b> and socialism; and over the place of the diaspora in African politics.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
